---
eleventyExcludeFromCollections: true
permalink: "/sitemap.xml"
---
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
{#
  The `<?xml ...?>` declaration above must be the very first thing this
  file writes -- XML requires it at byte zero, before even a comment, and
  the front-matter/body boundary above already supplies the one newline
  that has to follow it. Every explanatory comment therefore lives here,
  after the declaration, rather than before it (an earlier draft put a
  comment first and produced a leading blank line ahead of `<?xml ...?>`,
  which every XML parser -- `xml.etree.ElementTree` included -- rejects).

  Task 10: a plain, hand-written sitemap -- no new dependency (no
  `@11ty/eleventy-plugin-sitemap`), because `collections.all` plus one
  exclusion flag already says everything a sitemap needs to here.

  Every generated page appears below except the two field-presence archive
  filters (`archives-filter.njk`'s own `/archives/recordings/` and
  `/archives/discussions/`, marked `sitemap: false` in that template's own
  front matter -- see its comment for the full reasoning): each shows
  exactly the same past editions already listed, in full, on `/archives/`
  and on each year page under `/archives/<year>/`, and every one of those
  editions also has its own canonical page besides. A crawler gains
  nothing from a second and third ordering of a list it has already
  indexed once. The filters stay fully reachable for a visitor regardless
  -- the archive's own filter bar links to both (D-18, "filterable without
  JavaScript") -- this only decides what a crawler is pointed at, never
  what exists.

  Phase 7 task 5 added a second exclusion for the identical reason, a
  different shape: every survey page (`survey.njk`, also `sitemap: false`
  in its own front matter) is reached exactly one way, a link in an
  e-mail sent after the event to people this project already has a
  reason to write to -- a search engine sending a stranger there answers
  no question anyone searched for. Also fully reachable at its own real
  address for anyone who holds the link.

  Year pages (`archives-year.njk`) are kept in, unlike the two filters
  above: a year is a real search intent ("<organisation> talks 2025")
  that the full archive and the filters do not answer as directly, the
  same way a blog's own year/month archive commonly stays in its sitemap
  while a sort-order or in-stock filter does not.

  `p.url` is Eleventy's own unprefixed page address (e.g. `/events/mrg-05/`)
  -- `absoluteUrl` (site/.eleventy.js) turns it into this project's real
  published address in one step. Never `p.url | url | absoluteUrl` here:
  chaining the two would double the prefix.

  No `<lastmod>`: nothing in `events.json` records when a page's *content*
  was last edited (only when the edition happens), and inventing one from
  the clock at build time would make this file churn on every rebuild for
  a reason that has nothing to do with what changed -- the sitemap
  specification does not require it.
#}
<urlset xmlns="http://www.sitemaps.org/schemas/sitemap/0.9">
{%- for p in collections.all %}
{%- if p.data.sitemap != false %}
  <url><loc>{{ p.url | absoluteUrl }}</loc></url>
{%- endif %}
{%- endfor %}
</urlset>
